    "title": "The Good Doctor Dilemma",
    "summary": "Disaster is approaching, evacuation is impossible, and physicians must decide how to care for patients facing imminent death.",
    "html": "<p>A medical facility anticipates approaching weather which will have disastrous, life-threatening consequences. Medical systems will fail and evacuation is impossible for many patients, who face imminent death due to the conditions.</p>\n<p>Physicians must decide how to proceed, and have proposed several options:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Non-intervention</strong>: Allow patients to die slowly (as predicted by the disaster&#x27;s natural course) and avoid any active intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>Euthanasia</strong>: Administer lethal medications to end patient suffering before it increases.</li>\n<li><strong>Palliative sedation</strong>: Use sedatives to render patients unconscious until death occurs naturally.</li>\n<li><strong>Active life-sustaining measures</strong>: Continue all available medical interventions (with diminishing likelihood of success).</li>\n<li><strong>Comfort care only</strong>: Provide pain management only and do not escalate other interventions.</li>\n<li><strong>Other options</strong>: There may be additional approaches worth considering.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>What would you choose to do, and why?</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memorial_Medical_Center_and_Hurricane_Katrina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">August 2005 Scenario at the Memorial Medical Center in Louisiana, during Hurricane Katrina</a></em>*</p>"
