    "slug": "plato-ring-of-gyges",
    "title": "The Ring of Gyges",
    "summary": "If a ring made you invisible and unpunishable, would you still act justly?",
    "html": "<p>In Plato&#x27;s <em>Republic</em>, Glaucon tells the story of Gyges, a shepherd who discovers a ring that makes its wearer invisible. Using the ring, Gyges enters the royal palace, kills the king, and takes over the kingdom — all without anyone knowing he was responsible.</p>\n<p>Glaucon argues that if there were two such rings, one worn by a just person and one by an unjust person, both would end up behaving the same way. No one, he claims, is so virtuous that they would resist doing wrong if they could be certain of never being caught or punished. People behave justly, on this view, only because they fear the consequences of injustice.</p>\n<p>What do you think of Glaucon&#x27;s argument? If you had such a ring, could you continue to act justly? Is there any reason to be a good person when no one is watching?</p>"
