    "title": "The Tortured Child",
    "summary": "Would you build a perfectly happy society if it required the endless torture of one child?",
    "html": "<p>Suppose you were called upon to rebuild human society so that everyone in the world would be happy and would find peace and tranquility. There is a way to do this, but you know that it requires you to torture just one single creature: a child.</p>\n<p>The child must be locked in a small outhouse somewhere and remain in torture and desparation. However, through the child&#x27;s tears, you can build a society where everyone else is happy.</p>\n<p><em>Would you agree to do set up society this way?</em></p>\n<hr>\n<p class=\"dilemma-source\">Source: Ursula K. Le Guin, <a href=\"https://gizmodo.com/ursula-k-le-guin-fyodor-dovstoevsky-and-the-comfort-1719605127\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas</a></p>\n<p class=\"dilemma-source\">Source: Fyodor Dostoyevsky, <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28054/pg28054.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Brothers Karamazov</a> (&quot;Imagine that you are creating a fabric of human destiny...&quot;)</p>"
