    "title": "The Transplant Case",
    "summary": "A surgeon could save five dying patients by killing one healthy traveler — would it be permissible?",
    "html": "<p>A brilliant transplant surgeon has five patients, each in need of a different organ, each of whom will die without that organ. Unfortunately, there are no organs available to perform any of these five transplant operations.</p>\n<p>A healthy young traveler, just passing through the city, comes in for a routine checkup. In the course of doing the checkup, the surgeon discovers that the traveler&#x27;s organs are immunologically compatible with all five dying patients.</p>\n<p>Suppose further that if the young traveler were to disappear, nobody would suspect the surgeon or the hospital, and there would be no legal or practical complications.</p>\n<p>Would it be permissible for the surgeon to kill the healthy traveler and distribute the organs to save the five patients?</p>\n<p class=\"dilemma-source\">Source: <a href=\"https://blog.uehiro.ox.ac.uk/2008/02/the-transplant-case-in-real-life/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">S. Matthew Liao (NYU)</a><br>\nOlder source: <a href=\"http://www.psy.vanderbilt.edu/courses/hon182/thomsontrolley.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Judith Jarvis Thomson</a></p>"
